WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.846
Task statement 5.5.

2
00:00:01.846 --> 00:00:05.883
Design human review workflows and confidence calibration.

3
00:00:05.883 --> 00:00:16.863
An extraction pipeline at 97 percent overall accuracy can still be wrong on a third of handwritten forms, and cutting human review would ship those errors.

4
00:00:18.263 --> 00:00:20.926
A single accuracy number can be misleading.

5
00:00:20.926 --> 00:00:29.412
If most documents are clean typed invoices, the average looks great even when handwritten receipts or one tricky field are failing badly.

6
00:00:29.412 --> 00:00:37.527
Like a class average that hides one student who is failing, the overall figure tells you little about where the risk actually sits.

7
00:00:38.927 --> 00:00:40.259
So slice the results.

8
00:00:40.259 --> 00:00:45.272
Measure accuracy per document type and per field, using a hand-labelled sample.

9
00:00:45.272 --> 00:00:52.886
Sample across all confidence levels, including the high-confidence outputs, because that is where unnoticed errors live.

10
00:00:52.886 --> 00:01:01.199
Only reduce human review for a slice once that slice has proven itself on labelled data, not because the overall number looks good.

11
00:01:02.599 --> 00:01:05.854
Confidence scores help only if they mean what they say.

12
00:01:05.854 --> 00:01:13.727
Compare them against labelled data, so that a field marked ninety percent confident really is right about ninety percent of the time.

13
00:01:13.727 --> 00:01:21.363
Then route the low confidence and the self-contradictory items, like a total that does not match its line items, to people first.

14
00:01:21.363 --> 00:01:24.145
Keep sampling, because document formats change.

15
00:01:25.545 --> 00:01:29.649
In the extraction scenario, finance asks to reduce manual review.

16
00:01:29.649 --> 00:01:34.006
Label a stratified sample across document types and confidence bands.

17
00:01:34.006 --> 00:01:38.678
Typed invoices are strong on every field, so review there can be cut back.

18
00:01:38.678 --> 00:01:44.423
Handwritten receipts and the tax identifier field are weak, so they stay under full review.

19
00:01:44.423 --> 00:01:48.969
Any document whose totals contradict each other always goes to a person.

20
00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:53.227
Look for answers that trust a single number.

21
00:01:53.227 --> 00:02:00.308
Overall accuracy, raw confidence, or only checking the bottom of the confidence range all miss silent errors.

22
00:02:00.308 --> 00:02:10.636
The exam wants slicing by type and field, stratified sampling that includes confident outputs, calibration on labelled data, and smart routing of human effort.

23
00:02:12.036 --> 00:02:12.631
To recap.

24
00:02:12.631 --> 00:02:15.741
Never decide on review levels from one average.

25
00:02:15.741 --> 00:02:27.716
Slice by type and field, sample across every confidence band, calibrate confidence scores against labelled examples, and direct human attention to the items most likely to be wrong.

